1
00:00:00,439 --> 00:00:03,770
Evaluating an agent rigorously
is just as hard as building it.

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:08,848
IncidentBench physically separates public
evidence from private evaluator oracles;

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,625
labels are only unlocked
after a run persists its state

4
00:00:12,913 --> 00:00:16,494
and generates a matching
SHA-256 completion receipt,

5
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:19,379
and every technical failure
stays in the denominator.

6
00:00:19,649 --> 00:00:23,970
When we moved our project onto the official
DeepSeek API for full-scale testing,

7
00:00:24,281 --> 00:00:26,157
we uncovered a critical latent bug:

8
00:00:26,591 --> 00:00:30,993
Single and Full crashed on 8 out
of 12 slots during verification!

9
00:00:31,442 --> 00:00:34,205
Tracing the event logs
revealed a subtle hidden bug:

10
00:00:34,616 --> 00:00:38,698
ReviewOutput enforces a cross-field
rule called leading_review_consistency,

11
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:42,380
but structured_output.py stripped
that custom error code into

12
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:45,511
a generic $: validation_error,

13
00:00:46,019 --> 00:00:49,415
leaving the model completely blind
during its one repair attempt.

14
00:00:49,741 --> 00:00:54,241
After whitelisting the safe error code,
passing an explicit static repair hint,

15
00:00:54,659 --> 00:00:57,680
and documenting the invariant
in the verifier prompt —

16
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:02,533
verified by this new unit test—all 9
previously crashed slots recovered 9 out of 9,

17
00:01:02,928 --> 00:01:05,578
and across our subsequent 144-slot full run,

18
00:01:05,945 --> 00:01:08,638
structured output crashes
dropped to literally zero.
